{"step_index":144,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T18:40:11Z","thinking":"**Exploring Graph Domination Relations**\n\nInitially, search results were unhelpful, prompting a shift toward a mathematical perspective on the problem. Considering established knowledge in graph domination theory, identified key resources: *Fundamentals of Domination in Graphs* and *Total Domination in Graphs*. *Total Domination in Graphs* may contain relevant relationships with the diameter and radius, which are key graph invariants. The relationship between diameter, radius, and total domination number is the current focus.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/.venv/bin/python3 -c \\\"\\nimport networkx as nx\\nimport pulp\\n\\ndef gamma_t(n):\\n    P = nx.path_graph(n)\\n    prob = pulp.LpProblem('TDS', pulp.LpMinimize)\\n    nodes = list(P.nodes())\\n    x = {v: pulp.LpVariable(f'x_{v}', cat=pulp.LpBinary) for v in nodes}\\n    prob += pulp.lpSum(x.values())\\n    for v in nodes:\\n        prob += pulp.lpSum(x[u] for u in P.neighbors(v)) >= 1\\n    prob.solve(pulp.PULP_CBC_CMD(msg=False))\\n    return int(round(pulp.value(prob.objective)))\\n\\nfor n in range(2, 25):\\n    rad = (n) // 2  # radius of P_n\\n    gt = gamma_t(n)\\n    print(f'n={n:2d}: rad={rad:2d}, gamma_t={gt:2d}, diff={gt - rad}')\\n\\\"\"","Cwd":"\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_2a2555cc\"","WaitMsBeforeAsync":"5000","toolAction":"\"Computing gamma_t of paths\"","toolSummary":"\"Compute gamma_t of paths up to 25\""}}]}
